In 2016, the U.S. Air Force's X-37 orbital test vehicle was hijacked by hackers and reprogrammed to crash into the International Space Station. Aerospace Corp. contractor Aleksander Orzeł recruited an ex-CIA operative by the name of Jack Ryder to infiltrate the ISS and deactivate the rogue X-37 before it collided with the outpost. Ryder accepted the mission, boarded the ISS, and made his way to the airlock that housed the X-37. There, he dismantled the drone's onboard computer in order to regain control of it. After doing so, Ryder piloted the unmanned shuttle to a safe distance from the rest of the orbiting station, at which point his mission was complete.^{[1]}</s>

